its consumer. Either delete it (and the module docstring's `:func:`scan_tree`` reference) so Chunk 03 adds it with its doctor consumer and tests, or pin it now: a fixture tree carrying P1 and P3 listed in rank order, a clean fixture with `scanned > 0` and no sites, and a waived site absent.", "files": ["plugin/lib/test_scope.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "the one new public function in plugin/lib/test_scope.py that no test or caller reaches", "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Ordinal-keyed clock sites let a new unwaived read hide behind an existing waived one", "recommendation": "test-clock-import and test-clock-fake key a site by (path[, function], api, ordinal). Base sites are subtracted before waivers are applied, and only on the head side. So if the base has `A = time.time()  # prawduct:allow prawduct/test-scope -- reason` and the head inserts `B = time.time()` above it, B takes ordinal 1 and is matched away as pre-existing, A becomes ordinal 2, is waived, and nothing is reported. The same mismatch also puts the line of a legitimately reported new site on the old call when the new one is inserted first. A fix that closes the false negative: drop waived sites on BOTH sides before matching. Boundary-rated (WARNING), so it is an observation here. It would ride any planned commit to this module.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A clock read inside a nested function is reported twice", "recommendation": "`_enclosing_functions` yields both `outer` and `outer.inner`, and the test-clock-fake loop calls `_calls_outside_functions(stmt)` on each statement of `outer`'s body. When that statement is the nested `def inner` itself, the function node is the root, not a skipped child, so its body's calls are attributed to `outer` as well as to `outer.inner`. Consolidation merges the two by path:line into one entry with two details, so the effect is a duplicated detail, not two findings. Skip FunctionDef/AsyncFunctionDef/Lambda statements when iterating `fn.body`.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Colocated non-Python tests are silently passed, not reported unchecked", "recommendation": "`is_test_path` recognises a non-Python test file only under a directory named tests/test/testing/__tests__/spec/specs. Go's `foo_test.go`, JS/TS `*.test.ts`/`*.spec.ts` beside their source, and .NET's `MyApp.Tests/` project folders all fall outside that, so they get no `unchecked` entry. The acceptance criterion 'a non-Python changed test file produces an `unchecked` entry, not silence' and the module docstring's 'never silently passed' then hold only for test-directory layouts. Widen the file-name test per suffix (`_test.go`, `.test.`/`.spec.` infixes, a `*.Tests` directory segment), or narrow the docstring's claim to what is checked.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "First-party detection sees only root and src/ packages", "recommendation": "`_first_party` counts only `<name>/__init__.py` and `<name>.py` at the root or under `src/`. Monorepo layouts (`packages/foo/src/foo`, `backend/app/`), namespace packages with no `__init__.py`, and sys.path-shimmed trees like this repo's own `plugin/lib` are invisible, so a root conftest importing them yields nothing. The plan records this as an Inferred rule for the boundary to judge. Noted so the boundary review has the false-negative population in hand, not as a change for this chunk.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The production module's file name is pytest-shaped", "recommendation": "`plugin/lib/test_scope.py` matches `test_*.py`, so `is_test_path` classifies the detector itself as a test file, and so would any collector run over `plugin/` (only `testpaths = [\"tests\"]` keeps pytest off it). Harmless today. A non-test name (`test_determinism.py` has the same problem; something like `determinism_scope.py` does not) removes the self-classification.", "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Finding text and the waiver row cite a doc that does not exist yet", "recommendation": "Every detail string, the `unchecked` text and the new `test-scope` row in `plugin/docs/waivers.md` cite `docs/test-determinism.md`, which is Chunk 02's deliverable and absent from this tree. That is fine while 02 is in flight. The integration step should confirm the file exists under that path before the boundary review. Also confirm how a consumer reader resolves a bare `docs/\u2026` path in finding text, since in a product repo it names the product's own `docs/`.", "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A few detector branches are unpinned", "recommendation": "The blob-read failure return in `check` (only the listing failure is tested), the unparseable-base path (every head site counts as new), `ast.With`-nested module imports, and `import x as y` alias resolution for the ambient APIs have no test. Test-quality level, so boundary WARNING at most. Worth one case each if this module is reopened for the observations above.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Shipped comments cite the owner-ruling date", "recommendation": "The SEVERITY-table comment in record_lint.py and two test docstrings say 'owner ruling 2026-11-24'. That is review history in shipped text. The rule it records is already the table's value, and the plan holds the ruling. Deleting the date clause is the remedy, if the line is touched anyway.", "oid": "O-8"}], "duration_seconds": 720, "scope": "test-determinism", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01"], "chunks_proposed": ["01"], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {"01": {"paths": ["plugin/docs/waivers.md", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py", "plugin/lib/test_scope.py"], "paths_more": 0, "commits": []}}, "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"re